WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.907
This is Coding Exercise 1.

00:00:01.907 --> 00:00:08.062
You need one row containing three different counts from admissions: all admissions, completed admissions,

00:00:08.062 --> 00:00:13.597
and unique patients. Think carefully about which COUNT expression answers each question.

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:15.787
One measure should count every row.

00:00:15.787 --> 00:00:18.377
One should count only known discharge times.

00:00:18.377 --> 00:00:21.402
One should count distinct patient identifiers.

00:00:21.402 --> 00:00:25.432
Complete the SQL directly in the browser and select Run and Check.

00:00:25.432 --> 00:00:30.782
Before you run it, predict which of the three numbers should be largest and which should be smallest.

00:00:30.782 --> 00:00:36.632
If the SQL runs but does not pass, inspect the column aliases and the meaning of each count.

00:00:36.632 --> 00:00:39.797
Do not replace the query with a memorized answer.

00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:43.872
Use the definitions from the COUNT section to correct your work.

00:00:43.872 --> 00:00:49.927
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:49.927 --> 00:00:52.147
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:52.147 --> 00:00:57.297
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:57.297 --> 00:01:01.917
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:07.122
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:07.122 --> 00:01:13.487
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:13.487 --> 00:01:15.777
not only whether the statement executes.
